# ==============================================================================
# The 6809s' E and Q must be on global clock networks.
#
# mc6809i has no system clock: every sequential element in it is clocked by E or
# Q. The fitter left E driving 696 clock loads on ordinary routing ("Global: no"
# in the fit report) while clk_74a and clk_74b got GCLK6 and GCLK14. A 696-load
# clock spread across the fabric on regular routing has large and uncontrolled
# skew, so registers at opposite ends of the chip see the edge at different
# times -- which is how a CPU ends up mostly working and intermittently
# computing the wrong thing.
#
# This is a hardware-only fault by construction: a simulator has no routing
# delay, so no amount of Verilator will show it.
# ==============================================================================

# Quartus defaults to fixing hold only on I/O paths and minimum-TPD paths, so
# a core-internal hold violation is left alone -- that left a 20 ps hold
# failure at the fast/0C corner inside jt51's RAM-mapped shift register
# (jt51_op|jt51_sh:out_padding -> altshift_taps data-in). A hold violation
# is a real silicon failure, not lost margin, so let the fitter pad every
# path, not just the pins.
set_global_assignment -name OPTIMIZE_HOLD_TIMING "ALL PATHS"
